## Orchestrator + specialists pattern

The canonical 2026 architecture (Anthropic + industry consensus):

- **Orchestrator agent** — broad, strategic, keeps the global context. Never codes directly; decides who codes what.
- **Specialist subagents** — narrow, reliable, isolated context. Implementer, spec-reviewer, code-quality-reviewer, research fetcher, etc.
- **Message-passing, not shared state.** The orchestrator constructs exactly the context each subagent needs and passes it in the prompt. Subagents do **not** see the orchestrator's session history.
- **A subagent returns a summary + a pointer to its artifacts, never its full trace.** The orchestrator receives a short result (status + key decisions + paths to files/commits/notes the subagent wrote), not the subagent's raw tool calls and reasoning. Piping a subagent's whole transcript back into the orchestrator defeats the entire point of context isolation and re-pollutes the global context.
- **2–4 agents total is the sweet spot.** > 5 usually means the task is poorly decomposed.

### Model selection per role

Use the cheapest model that can handle the role. Concrete heuristic:

| Role | Model tier |
|---|---|
| Orchestrator (strategic, multi-turn coordination) | **Most capable** (Claude Opus / GPT-5-class) |
| Implementer on mechanical, well-specified tasks (1–2 files) | **Fast cheap** (Composer, Sonnet, etc.) |
| Implementer on integration / multi-file / judgment calls | **Standard** (Sonnet) |
| Reviewer (spec compliance and code quality) | **Standard or most capable** — reviewers must catch errors implementer missed |
| Research fetcher | **Cheap** with Context7 MCP |
| Architecture / design proposal | **Most capable** |

Wrong model selection is the #1 cost inefficiency. Do not default to Opus for everything.

